  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13928579/assets/aad161b50958b85f30421bf7420faf87ff4dc291ca1bb6d233626d67a42885ba.png)\n\nWhich of the following polynomials could be the function shown in the diagram above?",
  "worked_solution": "Let's call $P(x)$ the polynomial shown in the diagram.\n\nThe end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is odd, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is positive.\n\n- the $y$-intercept is $3,$ which means that $P(0)=3.$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, the only one that satisfies all three criteria is $y=2{x}^{5}-6{x}^{2}+3.$",
